# SYNOPSIS

```text
/svc/9gate/key
/svc/9gate/ctl
/svc/9gate/status
/svc/9gate/n/<name>
```

# DESCRIPTION

9gate speaks 9P2000 (9p(5)) between nodes, and is the only thing on a node that does but for cpu(8), which serves one terminal: it exports a subtree of its namespace to other nodes and imports theirs, as Plan 9's exportfs and import do (gate(2)).
Every connection between nodes is sealed by Noise IK (noise(2)), with the prologue `9gate` and empty payloads: the caller names the key of the node it calls, and the node answers only a caller whose key it trusts or the swarm file lists.
A plain listener serves peers that speak no Noise, Linux's v9fs and 9front: 9P2000 alone on the connection, without authentication, to callers from the addresses it allows.
It reaches the network through its namespace's `/net`, the stack's (ip(4)) with cs's (ndb(8)), and exports from its own namespace, which holds the node's devices at `/dev`, devmgr's (devmgr(8)): a listener whose root is `/dev` lets another node mount them.
It also holds the node's volume at `/data`, where the node has one (fs(4)), which a swarm file node exports (swarm(4)).

This node's key is factotum's (factotum(4)): 9gate's manifest grants it `proto=noise-ik`, and 9gate runs each handshake through factotum's sessions, passing the messages, so it never holds the key.
Nothing listens until a line in `ctl` says so.

: `key`
  This node's public key, 64 hex digits and a newline: what a caller names to reach it.
: `ctl`
  Takes one line a write: `trust`, `listen`, `plain` or `import`, below.
  A line it can't take fails with `bad arg in system call`; a listener or import already made fails with `file already exists`.
  Dialing and announcing take time, so `ctl` answers at once and `status` says how each went.
: `status`
  A line for each trusted key (`trust` <key>), each listener (`listen` <address> <root> and `announcing`, `listening` or `failed:` and why), each plain listener (`plain` <address> <root> <allow>... and its state, as a listener's), each caller connected to a plain listener (`caller` <address> <caller's address>`!`<port>, then each name it attached as, in which a space or control character reads as `?`) and each import (`import` <name> <address> and `dialing`, `ready` or `failed:` and why).
: `n/`<name>
  An imported tree, as the other node serves it.

The lines `ctl` takes:

: `trust` <key>
  A caller with this public key may connect.
  So may any member of the swarm file, whatever its `roles` (ndb(6)): cs is asked at each call, so a member added or taken out counts from the first call after cs rereads the file.
: `listen` <address> <root>
  Announces <address>, a dial string such as `tcp!*!564` (dial(2)), and exports <root> of 9gate's namespace to each trusted caller that connects.
: `plain` <address> <root> <allow>...
  Announces <address> and exports <root> in plain 9P2000, with no Noise and no authentication, to callers from an address or prefix in <allow>: `192.168.0.25`, `192.168.0.0/24`, `fe80::/10`.
  Any other caller is hung up before anything is read.
  The host in <address> is `*`, any address of this node's, or one address, and then a call to another is hung up; it can't be a name.
  The dialect is `9P2000` whatever the caller asks, and Tauth is refused, so 9front mounts with `-n` and Linux with `trans=tcp,version=9p2000`.
  The name a caller attaches as is shown in `status` and trusted for nothing: every file is `none`'s, as on any export (gate(2)).
: `import` <address> [<key>] <name> [<aname>]
  Dials <address>, expecting the node whose public key is <key>, and mounts its tree <aname> (its whole export, without one) at `n/`<name>.
  Without <key>, the address's host is a member of the swarm file, `tcp!board!564`, and its entry's `key` is the one (ndb(6)); a host with none fails the import.

Bind an import into a namespace to use it: a walk into `n/`<name> passes through 9gate's tree, and once a client has a channel to the import, its reads and writes go to the import alone.

# EXAMPLES

On a node whose key is `5c3f…`, export `/lib` to the node with key `a91e…`:

```rc
echo trust a91e… >/svc/9gate/ctl
echo listen tcp!*!564 /lib >/svc/9gate/ctl
```

On that node, import it and read from it:

```rc
echo import tcp!10.0.2.15!564 5c3f… lib >/svc/9gate/ctl
cat /svc/9gate/status
cat /svc/9gate/n/lib/ndb/local
```

Export this node's devices to the node with key `a91e…`, which mounts them at `n/board`:

```rc
echo trust a91e… >/svc/9gate/ctl
echo listen tcp!*!565 /dev >/svc/9gate/ctl
```

```rc
echo import tcp!192.168.0.24!565 5c3f… board >/svc/9gate/ctl
cat /svc/9gate/n/board/sdN0/ctl
```

Export `/lib` in plain 9P2000 to the dev machine at 192.168.0.30, and mount it there from Linux:

```rc
echo plain tcp!*!564 /lib 192.168.0.30 >/svc/9gate/ctl
```

```text
mount -t 9p -o trans=tcp,port=564,version=9p2000 192.168.0.24 /mnt/node
```

Or from 9front, without authentication and allowing creation:

```rc
srv -nc tcp!192.168.0.24!564 node /n/node
```

